"libvirtd runs as root and opens /dev/kvm to issue KVM ioctls that create virtual machines, allocate vCPUs, set up memory regions, and control device assignment. Without this devnode the daemon cannot access hardware\u2011assisted virtualization, so the sole\u2011owner element dev.kvm is required for its core workflow.\n\nAn attacker who can influence libvirtd\u2019s KVM ioctl input can trigger an out\u2011of\u2011bounds write in the kvm ioctl handler, achieving arbitrary kernel memory corruption. This primitive yields privilege escalation from the libvirtd process (uid\u202f0) to full kernel code execution, as reflected in the kvm\u2011ioctl CVE cluster.\n\nThe surface can be removed by disabling KVM acceleration in libvirtd/QEMU and letting QEMU use its TCG software emulator instead of /dev/kvm. This preserves the ability to start and manage VMs but incurs substantial CPU overhead, slowing guest execution and increasing host load."